## Linux DRM - Code Flow of open() and ioctl()
```require
- Kernel: Based on Linux v6.19 source
- Library: libdrm 2.4.129
- Prerequisites: Linux DRM Part 2: Core Initialization and Driver Registration
```
### Introduction
| Part | Topic |
|---|---|
| Part 1 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-26-post-linux-drm-01-architecture">Architecture and the Big Picture</a> |
| Part 2 | <a href="/posts/content/2026-10-27-post-linux-drm-02-register">Core Initialization and Driver Registration</a> |

In Part 2, we looked at the process of creating nodes such as `/dev/dri/card0`. This post traces the kernel functions executed the moment a user uses that node—specifically through <b>`open()`</b> and <b>`ioctl()`</b>. Finally, we will look at an example performing the same tasks in user space using libdrm.  


```adcard
```

### Code Flow 3: open()
When a user calls `open("/dev/dri/card0")`, the kernel looks up the <b>stub fops</b> previously registered with major number 226. The stub does not perform real work itself; instead, it <b>swaps out the file operations table with the driver's own</b>.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c - drm_stub_open() (L1188-1216)"
}
---
static int drm_stub_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
{
	const struct file_operations *new_fops;
	struct drm_minor *minor;
	int err;

	DRM_DEBUG("\n");

	minor = drm_minor_acquire(&drm_minors_xa, iminor(inode));  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(minor))
		return PTR_ERR(minor);

	new_fops = fops_get(minor->dev->driver->fops);
	if (!new_fops) {
		err = -ENODEV;
		goto out;
	}

	replace_fops(filp, new_fops);  // [!code highlight]
	if (filp->f_op->open)  // [!code highlight]
		err = filp->f_op->open(inode, filp);  // [!code highlight]
	else
		err = 0;

out:
	drm_minor_release(minor);

	return err;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c#L1188-L1216" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_drv.c L1188-L1216</a> (Linux v6.19)

```mermaid
{
    "title": "open(\"/dev/dri/card0\") Processing Flow"
}
---
sequenceDiagram
    participant U as User Process
    participant V as VFS (chrdev 226)
    participant S as drm_stub_open
    participant O as drm_open / drm_open_helper
    U->>V: open("/dev/dri/card0")
    V->>S: drm_stub_fops.open
    S->>S: drm_minor_acquire(iminor(inode))<br/>→ struct drm_minor
    S->>S: replace_fops(filp, driver->fops)
    S->>O: filp->f_op->open()  (= drm_open)
    O->>O: drm_file_alloc() → struct drm_file
    O->>O: If primary, drm_master_open()
    O-->>U: fd
```
The `.open` hook held in the driver's `fops` is `drm_open()`. This comes from a single macro line.  

```c
{
    "title": "include/drm/drm_gem.h - #define DRM_GEM_FOPS (L464-473)"
}
---
#define DRM_GEM_FOPS \
	.open		= drm_open,\
	.release	= drm_release,\
	.unlocked_ioctl	= drm_ioctl,\
	.compat_ioctl	= drm_compat_ioctl,\
	.poll		= drm_poll,\
	.read		= drm_read,\
	.llseek		= noop_llseek,\
	.mmap		= drm_gem_mmap, \
	.fop_flags	= FOP_UNSIGNED_OFFSET
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/include/drm/drm_gem.h#L464-L473" target="_blank">include/drm/drm_gem.h L464-L473</a> (Linux v6.19)

Inside this macro lie <b>all the entry points</b> we will see throughout this series: `.unlocked_ioctl = drm_ioctl` (ioctl), `.read = drm_read` (reading events), `.mmap = drm_gem_mmap` (buffer mapping), and `.poll = drm_poll`. Here are `drm_open()` and `drm_open_helper()`:  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c - drm_open() (L369-403)"
}
---
int drm_open(struct inode *inode, struct file *filp)
{
	struct drm_device *dev;
	struct drm_minor *minor;
	int retcode;

	minor = drm_minor_acquire(&drm_minors_xa, iminor(inode));  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(minor))
		return PTR_ERR(minor);

	dev = minor->dev;
	if (drm_dev_needs_global_mutex(dev))
		mutex_lock(&drm_global_mutex);

	atomic_fetch_inc(&dev->open_count);

	filp->f_mapping = dev->anon_inode->i_mapping;

	retcode = drm_open_helper(filp, minor);  // [!code highlight]
	if (retcode)
		goto err_undo;

	if (drm_dev_needs_global_mutex(dev))
		mutex_unlock(&drm_global_mutex);

	return 0;

err_undo:
	atomic_dec(&dev->open_count);
	if (drm_dev_needs_global_mutex(dev))
		mutex_unlock(&drm_global_mutex);
	drm_minor_release(minor);
	return retcode;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c#L369-L403" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c L369-L403</a> (Linux v6.19)

`drm_open()` resolves the minor and forwards it to `drm_open_helper()`.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c - drm_open_helper() (L316-355)"
}
---
int drm_open_helper(struct file *filp, struct drm_minor *minor)
{
	struct drm_device *dev = minor->dev;
	struct drm_file *priv;
	int ret;

	if (filp->f_flags & O_EXCL)
		return -EBUSY;
	if (!drm_cpu_valid())
		return -EINVAL;
	if (dev->switch_power_state != DRM_SWITCH_POWER_ON &&
	    dev->switch_power_state != DRM_SWITCH_POWER_DYNAMIC_OFF)
		return -EINVAL;
	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(!(filp->f_op->fop_flags & FOP_UNSIGNED_OFFSET)))
		return -EINVAL;

	drm_dbg_core(dev, "comm=\"%s\", pid=%d, minor=%d\n",
		     current->comm, task_pid_nr(current), minor->index);

	priv = drm_file_alloc(minor);  // [!code highlight]
	if (IS_ERR(priv))
		return PTR_ERR(priv);

	if (drm_is_primary_client(priv)) {
		ret = drm_master_open(priv);  // [!code highlight]
		if (ret) {
			drm_file_free(priv);
			return ret;
		}
	}

	filp->private_data = priv;  // [!code highlight]
	priv->filp = filp;

	mutex_lock(&dev->filelist_mutex);
	list_add(&priv->lhead, &dev->filelist);
	mutex_unlock(&dev->filelist_mutex);

	return 0;
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c#L316-L355" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c L316-L355</a> (Linux v6.19)

`drm_file_alloc()` allocates the state corresponding to a single `open()`. The `object_idr` mentioned earlier, the event queue, and other fields are initialized here.  


```adcard
```


```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c - drm_file_alloc() (L132-197)"
}
---
struct drm_file *drm_file_alloc(struct drm_minor *minor)
{
	/* ... */
	file = kzalloc(sizeof(*file), GFP_KERNEL);
	/* ... */
	file->minor = minor;

	file->authenticated = capable(CAP_SYS_ADMIN);  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&file->fbs);
	mutex_init(&file->fbs_lock);
	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&file->blobs);
	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&file->pending_event_list);
	INIT_LIST_HEAD(&file->event_list);
	init_waitqueue_head(&file->event_wait);
	file->event_space = 4096;  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	if (drm_core_check_feature(dev, DRIVER_GEM))
		drm_gem_open(dev, file);  // [!code highlight]

	if (drm_core_check_feature(dev, DRIVER_SYNCOBJ))
		drm_syncobj_open(file);  // [!code highlight]

	drm_prime_init_file_private(&file->prime);  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	if (dev->driver->open) {
		ret = dev->driver->open(dev, file);
		if (ret < 0)
			goto out_prime_destroy;
	}

	return file;
	/* ... */
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c#L132-L197" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_file.c L132-L197</a> (Linux v6.19)

<mark>drm_gem_open()</mark> initializes the handle table, which internally calls `idr_init_base(&file_private->object_idr, 1)`. This is the reason why handle numbers start from <b>1</b> (leaving 0 to represent "none"). Thanks to `filp->private_data = priv`, subsequent `ioctl`, `read`, and `mmap` calls can directly obtain `drm_file` from `file->private_data`.  

### Code Flow 4: ioctl()
`ioctl(fd, DRM_IOCTL_xxx, arg)` enters into <mark>drm_ioctl()</mark>, which serves as `fops->unlocked_ioctl`.  

```mermaid
{
    "title": "Dispatch Flow in drm_ioctl()"
}
---
flowchart TD
    A["ioctl(fd, DRM_IOCTL_xxx, arg)"] --> B["drm_ioctl()"]
    B --> C{"nr ≥ DRM_COMMAND_BASE<br/>(0x40) ?"}
    C -->|"Yes : Driver ioctl"| D["dev->driver->ioctls[nr - 0x40]"]
    C -->|"No : Core ioctl"| E["drm_ioctls[nr]"]
    D --> F["copy_from_user → kdata"]
    E --> F
    F --> G["drm_ioctl_kernel()"]
    G --> H{"drm_ioctl_permit()<br/>AUTH · MASTER · ROOT_ONLY · RENDER_ALLOW"}
    H -->|"Denied"| I["-EACCES"]
    H -->|"Permitted"| J["func(dev, kdata, file_priv)"]
    J --> K["copy_to_user → result"]
```
```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c - drm_ioctl() (L821-919)"
}
---
long drm_ioctl(struct file *filp,
	      unsigned int cmd, unsigned long arg)
{
	/* ... */
	unsigned int nr = DRM_IOCTL_NR(cmd);
	/* ... */
	dev = file_priv->minor->dev;

	if (drm_dev_is_unplugged(dev))
		return -ENODEV;

	if (DRM_IOCTL_TYPE(cmd) != DRM_IOCTL_BASE)
		return -ENOTTY;
	/* ... */
	is_driver_ioctl = nr >= DRM_COMMAND_BASE && nr < DRM_COMMAND_END;  // [!code highlight]

	if (is_driver_ioctl) {
		unsigned int index = nr - DRM_COMMAND_BASE;  // [!code highlight]

		if (index >= dev->driver->num_ioctls)
			goto err_i1;
		index = array_index_nospec(index, dev->driver->num_ioctls);
		ioctl = &dev->driver->ioctls[index];  // [!code highlight]
	} else {
		if (nr >= DRM_CORE_IOCTL_COUNT)
			goto err_i1;
		nr = array_index_nospec(nr, DRM_CORE_IOCTL_COUNT);
		ioctl = &drm_ioctls[nr];  // [!code highlight]
	}
	/* ... */
	func = ioctl->func;
	/* ... */
	if (copy_from_user(kdata, (void __user *)arg, in_size) != 0) {  // [!code highlight]
		retcode = -EFAULT;
		goto err_i1;
	}

	if (ksize > in_size)
		memset(kdata + in_size, 0, ksize - in_size);

	retcode = drm_ioctl_kernel(filp, func, kdata, ioctl->flags);  // [!code highlight]
	if (copy_to_user((void __user *)arg, kdata, out_size) != 0)  // [!code highlight]
		retcode = -EFAULT;
	/* ... */
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c#L821-L919" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L821-L919</a> (Linux v6.19)

Summarizing the core steps:  

1. Checks whether the `type` of the ioctl number is `'d'` (`DRM_IOCTL_BASE`).
2. If the number is <mark>0x40 or greater, it looks up the entry in the driver table</mark> (`dev->driver->ioctls`); otherwise, it looks in the <mark>core table</mark> (`drm_ioctls[]`).
3. Rather than trusting the size provided by the user, it calculates the buffer size using the <b>kernel definition (`ioctl->cmd`)</b> (`Do not trust userspace`).
4. After `copy_from_user()`, `drm_ioctl_kernel()` <b>checks permissions</b> and invokes the actual function.

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c - drm_ioctl_kernel() (L787-805)"
}
---
long drm_ioctl_kernel(struct file *file, drm_ioctl_t *func, void *kdata,
		      u32 flags)
{
	struct drm_file *file_priv = file->private_data;
	struct drm_device *dev = file_priv->minor->dev;
	int ret;

	drm_file_update_pid(file_priv);

	if (drm_dev_is_unplugged(dev))
		return -ENODEV;

	ret = drm_ioctl_permit(flags, file_priv);
	if (unlikely(ret))
		return ret;

	return func(dev, kdata, file_priv);
}
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c#L787-L805" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L787-L805</a> (Linux v6.19)

The core ioctl table is an array constructed using macros. Selecting only the entries we will encounter in this series yields the following list. The <b>flags</b> on the far right correspond to the permission rules mentioned earlier.  

```c
{
    "title": "drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c - drm_ioctls (L632-735)"
}
---
static const struct drm_ioctl_desc drm_ioctls[] = {
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_VERSION, drm_version, DRM_RENDER_ALLOW),  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_SET_MASTER, drm_setmaster_ioctl, 0),
	/* ... */
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_GEM_CLOSE, drm_gem_close_ioctl, DRM_RENDER_ALLOW),  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_MODE_GETRESOURCES, drm_mode_getresources, 0),
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_PRIME_HANDLE_TO_FD, drm_prime_handle_to_fd_ioctl, DRM_RENDER_ALLOW),  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_MODE_CREATE_DUMB, drm_mode_create_dumb_ioctl, 0),
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_MODE_MAP_DUMB, drm_mode_mmap_dumb_ioctl, 0),
	/* ... */
	DRM_IOCTL_DEF(DRM_IOCTL_MODE_ATOMIC, drm_mode_atomic_ioctl, DRM_MASTER),  // [!code highlight]
	/* ... */
};
```

> **Source** <a href="https://github.com/torvalds/linux/blob/v6.19/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c#L632-L735" target="_blank">drivers/gpu/drm/drm_ioctl.c L632-L735</a> (Linux v6.19)


